// Change: new rule
// Title: Application Whitelisting Bypass Via Microsoft.Workflow.Compiler.EXE
// Sigma ID: 3061a635-282a-4d59-8e62-0637949d8389
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: EinLamYe
// Date: 2026-06-29 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: execution, stealth, T1127, T1218
// Description: Detects execution of "Microsoft.Workflow.Compiler.exe", a deprecated and signed .NET Framework
//   utility that can compile and execute arbitrary C#/VB code provided in an XOML/XML input file.
//   Adversaries abuse it to run unsigned code and bypass application whitelisting (AWL). The utility
//   has no legitimate interactive use on modern endpoints, so any execution is highly suspicious.
// False positives:
//   - Rare legacy build/automation pipelines that still rely on Windows Workflow Foundation compilation
// References:
//   - https://lolbas-project.github.io/lolbas/Binaries/Microsoft.Workflow.Compiler/
//   - https://posts.specterops.io/arbitrary-unsigned-code-execution-vector-in-microsoft-workflow-compiler-exe-3d9294bc5efb
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/c5875f6ddd2f5087d0f0a63a34a2ed4468a9d328/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_lolbin_workflow_compiler.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "\\Microsoft.Workflow.Compiler.exe" or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName =~ "Microsoft.Workflow.Compiler.exe"
